---
name: type-ascription-list
description: "case _: List[?] holds for a List on the lanes that represent one distinctly"
backends: [int, jvm, v2]
---

# `case _: List[?]`

`BUGS.md` `type-ascription-tuple-and-set-arms-missing`. On the native lane a list is
`DataV("Cons"/"Nil")` and the type test compares the CONSTRUCTOR TAG to the type name, so
`case _: List[?]` — which names a type, not a constructor — could never match. The reference
interpreter has carried the equivalent arm forever
(`case _: Value.ListV => typeName == "List" || "Seq" || "Iterable"`); it was simply never brought
across.

```scalascript
def kind(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: List[?] => "list"
  case _ => "other"

println(kind(List(1, 2)))
println(kind(List()))
println(kind(Map("a" -> 1)))
println(kind("s"))
println(kind(7))

def firstOr(xs: List[Int], d: Int): Int = xs match
  case h :: t => h
  case _ => d
println(firstOr(List(4, 5), 0))
println(firstOr(List(), 0))
```

The empty list matters: it is `DataV("Nil")` rather than `"Cons"`, a different tag reaching the same
test. The constructor patterns at the end are the guard that widening the TYPE test did not disturb
the CONSTRUCTOR one — `case h :: t` still arrives with the tag as its own name and must keep going
through the ordinary path.

**`js` is absent, and not because of an oversight.** There a `List(...)` is `[...args]` and
`_setOf(...)` returns a plain array too, so List and Set are the same value on that lane and no arm
can separate them. See the entry for the split between the table gaps (closable) and that
representation gap (not).
